{"name":"Duchess Rose","hex":"#f7aa97","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"duchess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The wife or widow of a duke.","The female ruler of a duchy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To court or curry favour for political or business advantage; to flatter obsequiously."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duchess"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Duke","blocks":[{"text":"Duke is a male title either of a monarch ruling over a duchy, or of a member of royalty, or nobility. As rulers, dukes are ranked below emperors, kings, grand princes, grand dukes, and above sovereign princes. As royalty or nobility, they are ranked below grand dukes and above or below princes, depending on the country or specific title. The title comes from French duc, itself from the Latin dux, 'leader', a term used in republican Rome to refer to a military commander without an official rank, and later coming to mean the leading military commander of a province. In most countries, the word duchess is the female equivalent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg/330px-James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose"},{"title":"Rose (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Rose is the color halfway between red and magenta, whose hue corresponds to an angle of 330 degrees on the HSV/HSL color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg/330px-Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg/250px-Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg/250px-Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the 1260s Birger Jarl bore a ducal coronet and used the Latin title of Dux Sweorum (Latin for \"Duke of the Swedes\"); the design of his coronet combined those used by continental European and English dukes.[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABirger_jarl_(Forss%C3%A9n).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Bengt_Birgersson.JPG/500px-Bengt_Birgersson.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikime